            <ArticleTitle>Structural Biology-Inspired Discovery of Novel KRAS-PDEδ Inhibitors.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>9400-9406</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acs.jmedchem.7b01243</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Structural biology is a powerful tool for investigating the stereospecific interactions between a protein and its ligand. Herein, an unprecedented chiral binding pattern was observed for inhibitors of KRAS-PDEδ interactions. Virtual screening and X-ray crystallography studies revealed that two enantiomers of a racemic inhibitor could bind at different sites. Fragment-based drug design was used to identify highly potent PDEδ inhibitors that can be used as promising lead compounds for target validation and antitumor drug development.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
